 are a key consideration for investigational drugs\",\n            \"explanation\": \"Beyond metabolic enzymes, drug transporters such as OATP1B1, OAT1, OAT3, and MATE2-K play critical roles in the absorption, distribution, and elimination of drugs. Regulatory guidance indicates that if an investigational drug is a substrate of these transporters, its interaction with known inhibitors (e.g., OATP1B1 inhibitors) must be evaluated based on safety margins and therapeutic indices. This reflects a growing recognition that transporter-mediated DDIs can significantly impact drug exposure and toxicity, especially for renally cleared or hepatobiliary-excreted compounds. [Data: Relationships (33906, 33908, 33902, 33903, 33904); Entities (13593, 13158, 13159, 9882, 2983)]\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"丹参-derived compounds show complex and variable effects on CYP enzymes\",\n            \"explanation\": \"Research indicates that different components of Salvia miltiorrhiza—such as 丹参酮Ⅰ, 丹参酮ⅡA, 隐丹参酮, and 丹参素—exert divergent effects on various CYP isoforms. For instance, while 丹参素 induces CYP2C19, other tanshinones may induce CYP1A1, CYP1A2, CYP2B1, or CYP2E1. These inconsistent effects across experimental models (in vivo, in vitro, liver microsomes) complicate prediction of net clinical impact, highlighting the need for comprehensive herb-drug interaction profiling when using multi-component botanical products. [Data: Relationships (337); Entities (3366, 3357)]\"\n        }\n    ],\n    \"rating\": 6.5,\n    \"rating_explanation\": \"The community presents moderate-to-high impact due to the potential for clinically significant drug-drug interactions involving widely used herbal compounds and critical metabolic pathways.\"\n}", "refusal": null, "role": "assistant", "annotations": null, "audio": null, "function_call": null, "tool_calls": null, "provider_specific_fields": {"refusal": null}}, "provider_specific_fields": {}}], "created": 1782128388, "model": "qw